@page "/form"

<CatHead Title="Form layout">
    <code>.form-field</code> stacks a label, a control and a hint; <code>.field-grid</code> puts two
    side by side; <code>.form-section</code> groups them and <code>.form-actions</code> closes the
    form. The controls themselves each have their own page under <strong>Inputs</strong>.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.Fields" Title="Fields" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>Label, control, hint. The hint explains the consequence, not the control.</p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.TwoUpRows" Title="Two-up rows" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.field-grid</code> lays fields out side by side and collapses to one column when there
        is not room for two &mdash; no breakpoint to pick.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.SectionsActions" Title="Sections and actions" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.form-section</code> is a <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code> with the browser's border and
        inset legend removed. Use the real element &mdash; the legend is announced with every control
        in the group, which a heading above a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> is not &mdash; and put the
        fields in <code>.form-section-body</code>, because a <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code> is not a flex
        item and lands somewhere different in every engine if the fieldset is the flex container.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.ActionsAlignment" Title="Where the actions sit" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.form-actions</code> wraps instead of overflowing, so three buttons on a phone stack
        rather than pushing the commit button off-screen. <code>--split</code> puts a destructive
        action where it cannot be hit by aiming for <strong>Save</strong>; <code>--start</code> aligns
        the row to the leading edge, for a form embedded in a page rather than closing a dialog.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.SectionTitle" Title="A group that is not a fieldset" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.form-section-title</code> on a plain heading, for a region rather than a set of related
        controls. Same rule, without claiming a grouping that is not there &mdash; a
        <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code> tells a screen reader these answers belong together.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.WholeForm" Title="A whole form" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>Every piece on this page at once, as a starting point to paste and cut down.</p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.SettingsCard" Title="A settings card" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>Fields in a card, actions in a footer row.</p>
</CatExample>
